{
  "topic": "Geminid meteor shower",
  "language": "en",
  "format": "long",
  "category": "ciencia",
  "title": "The BEST Meteor Shower of the Year: the Geminids ✨",
  "hook": "This week the sky is getting ready for one of the best shooting star shows of the year.",
  "presenter": "perro",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "role": "hook",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_hook",
      "narration": "This week the sky is getting ready for one of the best shooting star shows of the year.",
      "on_screen_text": "The sky's best show",
      "highlight_words": [
        "show"
      ],
      "sfx": "impact",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_rain",
      "narration": "It's called the Geminid meteor shower, and its best moment arrives on the night of December thirteenth to fourteenth.",
      "on_screen_text": "December 13–14",
      "highlight_words": [
        "December"
      ],
      "sfx": "riser",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "sl_stars",
      "narration": "Under a dark sky you can see dozens per hour. I'm Leo, and today I'll explain where they come from and how to see them.",
      "on_screen_text": "Dozens per hour",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Dozens"
      ],
      "sfx": "pop",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_grain",
      "narration": "First surprise: a shooting star isn't a star. It's almost always a speck of dust, about the size of a grain of sand.",
      "on_screen_text": "A grain of sand",
      "highlight_words": [
        "sand"
      ],
      "sfx": "ding",
      "transition": "cut",
      "chapter": "What is a shooting star?"
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_grain",
      "narration": "Bigger ones, the size of a marble, make fireballs that light up the sky for an instant.",
      "on_screen_text": "Fireballs",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Fireballs"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_burn",
      "narration": "That speck enters the atmosphere at tens of kilometers per second, far faster than a bullet.",
      "on_screen_text": "Faster than a bullet",
      "highlight_words": [
        "bullet"
      ],
      "sfx": "whoosh",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_burn",
      "narration": "About a hundred kilometers up, it slams into the air so fast that it heats it until it glows.",
      "on_screen_text": "The air glows",
      "highlight_words": [
        "glows"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_burn",
      "narration": "That flash, lasting less than a second, is what we call a shooting star. The speck burns up high above.",
      "on_screen_text": "Less than a second",
      "highlight_words": [
        "second"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_rain",
      "narration": "So there's no danger at all: almost nothing reaches the ground, and what does is extremely fine dust.",
      "on_screen_text": "No danger",
      "highlight_words": [
        "danger"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_orbit",
      "narration": "So why so many in December? Most meteor showers come from dust shed by comets.",
      "on_screen_text": "Comet dust",
      "highlight_words": [
        "comets"
      ],
      "sfx": "ding",
      "transition": "cut",
      "chapter": "A stony asteroid"
    },
    {
      "id": "s11",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_orbit",
      "narration": "But the Geminids are special: they come from an asteroid called Phaethon, a name borrowed from Greek myths.",
      "on_screen_text": "They come from Phaethon",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Phaethon"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s12",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_orbit",
      "narration": "Phaethon circles the Sun about every year and a half, leaving a trail of dust along its path.",
      "on_screen_text": "A trail of dust",
      "highlight_words": [
        "trail"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s13",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_orbit",
      "narration": "Every December, Earth crosses that trail, and millions of specks plunge into our atmosphere.",
      "on_screen_text": "Earth crosses the trail",
      "highlight_words": [
        "crosses"
      ],
      "sfx": "whoosh",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s14",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_orbit",
      "narration": "That's why Phaethon is sometimes called a rock comet: it acts a bit like a comet, even though it looks like an asteroid.",
      "on_screen_text": "A rock comet",
      "highlight_words": [
        "rock"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s15",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "sol_surface",
      "narration": "Phaethon gets extremely close to the Sun. Scientists think the heat cracks it and breaks off that dust.",
      "on_screen_text": "The Sun cracks it",
      "highlight_words": [
        "cracks"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s16",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_radiant",
      "narration": "Now, how to see them. They all seem to come from one point in the sky, in the constellation Gemini.",
      "on_screen_text": "They come from Gemini",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Gemini"
      ],
      "sfx": "ding",
      "transition": "cut",
      "chapter": "How to watch"
    },
    {
      "id": "s17",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_radiant",
      "narration": "That's why they're called Geminids. But don't look only there: they appear anywhere in the sky.",
      "on_screen_text": "They show up everywhere",
      "highlight_words": [
        "everywhere"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s18",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_watch",
      "narration": "Find a spot far from city lights. The darker the sky, the more shooting stars you'll see.",
      "on_screen_text": "Away from the lights",
      "highlight_words": [
        "lights"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s19",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "sl_fade",
      "narration": "This year the Moon will be just a thin sliver at the peak, so the sky will be nice and dark.",
      "on_screen_text": "The Moon won't get in the way",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Moon"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s20",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_watch",
      "narration": "Lie back looking up and let your eyes adjust to the dark for about twenty minutes.",
      "on_screen_text": "Twenty minutes of patience",
      "highlight_words": [
        "patience"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s21",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_rain",
      "narration": "You don't need a telescope or binoculars: just your eyes, a blanket and a little patience.",
      "on_screen_text": "Just your eyes",
      "highlight_words": [
        "eyes"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s22",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_watch",
      "narration": "The best time is usually after midnight, when Gemini is highest in the sky.",
      "on_screen_text": "After midnight",
      "highlight_words": [
        "midnight"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s23",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_burn",
      "narration": "The Geminids are slower than other showers: they come in at about thirty-five kilometers per second.",
      "on_screen_text": "Slow… at 35 km/s",
      "highlight_words": [
        "35"
      ],
      "sfx": "ding",
      "transition": "cut",
      "chapter": "Surprising facts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s24",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_rain",
      "narration": "That's why many look long and bright, sometimes with flashes of color.",
      "on_screen_text": "Flashes of color",
      "highlight_words": [
        "color"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s25",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_rain",
      "narration": "If it's cloudy, don't worry: the shower lasts several days, so you can try another night nearby.",
      "on_screen_text": "Several nights",
      "highlight_words": [
        "nights"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s26",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_radiant",
      "narration": "They're visible from most of the world, though from the far south Gemini sits lower on the northern horizon.",
      "on_screen_text": "Visible almost everywhere",
      "highlight_words": [
        "everywhere"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s27",
      "role": "surprise",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_watch",
      "narration": "And a fun fact: space dust falls on Earth every day. Some of it might even be on your roof.",
      "on_screen_text": "Space dust on your roof",
      "highlight_words": [
        "roof"
      ],
      "sfx": "riser",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s28",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_watch",
      "narration": "Put your phone away: its bright screen makes your eyes lose their dark adaptation.",
      "on_screen_text": "Put the phone away",
      "highlight_words": [
        "phone"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s29",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "sl_stars",
      "narration": "So this week, turn off the lights, look up and make as many wishes as you like.",
      "on_screen_text": "Make your wishes",
      "highlight_words": [
        "wishes"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s30",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "sl_stars",
      "narration": "And if you want a challenge, count how many you see in an hour and compare with your friends.",
      "on_screen_text": "The counting challenge",
      "highlight_words": [
        "challenge"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s31",
      "role": "closing",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "met_outro",
      "narration": "Tell me in the comments how many shooting stars you saw.",
      "on_screen_text": "How many did you see?",
      "highlight_words": [
        "many"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    }
  ],
  "description": "On December 13–14 the Geminid meteor shower peaks. Here's what a shooting star is, why this shower comes from an asteroid, and how to watch it. Original 3D animation; sizes and distances are not to scale.",
